1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,487
Host: Welcome to Studio Notes. Today we're talking about the messy reality of freelance editing.

2
00:00:07,487 --> 00:00:10,812
Host: What is the biggest mistake you made when you started editing?

3
00:00:11,412 --> 00:00:17,748
Guest: I thought the secret was buying more plugins. But actually, my best project used none of them.

4
00:00:18,248 --> 00:00:24,312
Guest: Because the story was already there. I just had to cut out everything that was getting in its way.

5
00:00:28,312 --> 00:00:33,789
Host: That makes sense. Let's talk about the less glamorous part: working with clients.

6
00:00:34,789 --> 00:00:38,944
Host: What happened the first time a client asked you to make the video pop?

7
00:00:39,544 --> 00:00:46,311
Guest: I once spent an entire night adding transitions. Every cut had a different effect. I was so proud.

8
00:00:46,811 --> 00:00:52,311
Guest: In the end, the client sent it back and said: could you make it a little less popcorn?

9
00:00:53,011 --> 00:00:57,919
Host: Okay, that one got me. Sometimes the best transition is just a cut.

10
00:01:01,919 --> 00:01:06,810
Host: People often talk about speed. How do you actually finish a difficult edit?

11
00:01:07,410 --> 00:01:13,940
Guest: I never start with the timeline anymore. Instead, I write one sentence about what the viewer should feel.

12
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:23,380
Guest: For example, if the story is about taking a risk, every shot needs to help us feel that uncertainty. That's why quiet moments matter.

13
00:01:27,380 --> 00:01:30,987
Host: Do you have a story about a project that almost went wrong?

14
00:01:31,587 --> 00:01:37,747
Guest: Last year, my drive failed the night before delivery. I thought I had lost three weeks of work.

15
00:01:38,247 --> 00:01:48,382
Guest: Then I realized the assistant had kept a backup on a tiny old drive. We finished the film on time. Ever since, backups are part of my creative process.

16
00:01:49,382 --> 00:01:54,152
Host: That is a useful lesson to end on. Thanks for listening to Studio Notes.
